  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 AM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nLow pressure, currently over WI, will continue to track slowly east \nreaching northern Lk Huron by 18Z Tue. A quasi stationary boundary \nhas been responsible for showers and even a few storms, but so far \nthese have remained well north of our area. A cold front trails the \nlow into eastern IA. This will be our focus over the next 24 to 36 \nhours as it shifts slowly east and eventually stalls out as it \nbecomes parallel to the flow. We will remain entrenched in the warm \nsector of the system with one more day of well above normal \ntemperatures as highs climb into the 80s. Bit of mid/high level \ncloud cover may limit overall warming and keep temps from getting \ntoo out of hand. That being said, records at FWA (84 in 1975 and \n1899) and SBN (86 set in 1975) could be in jeopardy. \n\nIncreasing moisture begins to arrive west of the area along the cold \nfront with another weak wave taking shape across IL/IA and then \ndrifting east along the stalling front. Strong to locally severe \nstorms will develop where the greatest shear and instability exists \nlate afternoon into the evening hours. This activity should drift \neast and impact the area sometime overnight into early Wednesday, \nwith the RAP/HRRR faster (near/after 6Z) and NAM closer to 12Z. Have \nlimited pops to likely and during the overnight hours before a slow \ndownward trend in coverage and intensity is likely (unless the NAM \nworks out). Showalters indicate a plume of elevated instability, but \nlimited lapse rates and shear which should keep thunder chances on \nthe lower side. Precip chances should decrease during the afternoon \nand into Wed evening as the front washes out and wave moves away. \n\nFocus then shifts to the larger trough which will reside across the \nSW US. Models seem to have finally come into some agreement on \nphasing of northern and southern stream waves and eventual \ncyclogenesis with the sfc low tracking near/northwest of the area. \nShower and thunderstorm chances will increase again Thurs afternoon \ninto Thurs evening before dry slow works in late Thursday night. A \nbreezy day will be in store Friday as NW winds wrap around the low \nand usher in colder air for Fri and Sat. \n\nAny cold air should be brief as pattern begins to deamplify into \nnext week with a return to near or possibly above normal \ntemperatures.",
